may be considered \"different interaction\" and lower fees.\n\nCould be the intended candidate set is \"all ACIs that are possible for the merchant's transactions\" or \"ACI values in fee rules\". All A-G appear.\n\nExisting answer NexPay:3.49 maybe from a model where for each card scheme, pick preferred ACI (minimum total fee), then among schemes pick lowest? But per scheme minimum would be 0 for D/E/F/G; if exclude G (same) maybe still 0. If candidate only C? Then NexPay is min across schemes. The existing answer likely came from a particular interpretation:\n- Move fraud transactions to ACI C (maybe because C is the next ACI in some ordering?).\n- Compute fee per scheme for fraud tx with ACI C.\n- Choose NexPay because lowest 3.49.\nBut why C? Maybe they evaluated each ACI across all fraudulent tx and chose overall lowest \"cost\", then reported the card scheme of...? Let's see overall fraud subset costs under sum: E=77.68 lowest among alternatives (B=202.97, etc.), not C. Whole-month costs: E lowest. If using min model: B=54.21 but E=16.63 lowest. Under average also E lowest. So E would be preferred if evaluating all fraud across schemes. Existing answer not that.\n\nMaybe \"move fraudulent transactions towards a different ACI\" means not reassign ACI of existing transactions, but find the ACI with lowest fee *for each card scheme* and then choose scheme? Let's compute per-scheme per-ACI with a different model perhaps. The data: GlobalCard, NexPay, SwiftCharge, TransactPlus fraud tx all ACI G. For each scheme, find ACI among A-F minimizing fee, then choose scheme with lowest. Since many zero fees, tie. Maybe only consider ACIs with matching fee rules (nonzero? or applied?) Still D/E/F may zero due no rules matching. Let's inspect matching rules for fraud tx when ACI changed, per scheme.\n\nMaybe the fee model intended is not sum but something else where per-scheme minima differ. Existing \"NexPay:3.49\" might be coincidental to parent's attempt; we need derive intended.\n\nLet's understand fee rules patterns. Why do some ACI/scheme combinations have no matching fee rules? The fee dataset may be generated so each combination has many rules, but for ACI D/E/F/G and is_credit=True + specific MCC/account/capture etc maybe not. Let's count matching rules for Belles Jan per scheme/ACI under monthly buckets."}
