action* (POS/Ecommerce), which corresponds to ACI? Manual says ACI indicates transaction flow. \"by incentivizing users to use a different interaction\" perhaps means change `shopper_interaction` from Ecommerce to POS, and thus ACI from G to A/B? Or change to \"tokenized\" etc. The phrase \"different interaction\" might refer to shopper_interaction column. If we move fraud transactions from Ecommerce to POS, then ACI should become A or B (card present). Which of A/B preferred? Compute fees for fraud tx with ACI A or B, but perhaps also keep is_credit, etc. Under min model B could be cheaper for some schemes. Let's compute per scheme/total.\n\nQuestion 2697 wording: \"if we were to move the fraudulent transactions towards a different Authorization Characteristics Indicator (ACI) by incentivizing users to use a different interaction\". This likely means changing ACI from current G to another, not necessarily changing shopper_interaction in data. \"different interaction\" colloquial.\n\nMaybe candidate ACI must be compatible with `shopper_interaction` = Ecommerce. Valid card-not-present ACIs: D, E, F, G. Excluding current G, candidates D/E/F. Then sums:\n- GlobalCard D/E/F all 0 (no rules)\n- NexPay all 0\n- SwiftCharge D=6.98, E=5.64, F=0\n- TransactPlus D=90.69, E=72.04, F=94.61\nPreferred overall lowest = 0 for GlobalCard/NexPay D/E/F. Again not NexPay C.\n\nIf exclude zero-match (no rule found) as \"not applicable\", then SwiftCharge E=5.64, TransactPlus E=72.04, etc. NexPay has no D/E/F rules, so not applicable. Preferred SwiftCharge E=5.64 maybe. Not existing.\n\nMaybe instead the model for total fee is \"minimum matching fee\" (not sum), and candidate ACIs are all A-F. Then costs for fraud subset:\nA=81.81, B=54.21, C=81.04, D=36.62, E=16.63, F=33.56. Preferred E=16.63. Per scheme maybe. Existing no.\n\nAverage model: E=41.66. Max: F=61.05 (same as current G) etc. Still E/F.\n\nMaybe the cost associated is not sum over all fraud transactions, but average fee per